Canonical Grammar Data
===============================================================================

The normative language grammar is docs/design/specification.mec. This directory
contains its generated dependencies, rule identities, syntax schemas, and current
conformance inputs. Source history retains the completed parser migration and
qualification diaries; they are not ongoing update obligations.

Current responsibilities
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- ports.tsv maps specification grammar names to stable RuleIds, syntax node
  policies, semantic dispositions, and parser reachability. The rule and port
  registries are generated from this input.
- canonical-dependencies.tsv and grammar-component-boundaries.tsv describe the specification
  dependency graph. recursive-core.tsv identifies the recursive component.
- recursive-core-syntax-schema.tsv defines typed syntax structure and accessor contracts.
- recursive-core-certification.tsv and document-certification.tsv contain executable
  accepted, rejected, recovery, and typed-view expectations. These are live conformance data and generator inputs.
- recursive-core-semantic-errors.tsv contains current semantic-error witnesses.
  Runtime behavior and supported target outcomes belong to their owner tests. Syntax acceptance alone does
  not imply that every target can activate the resulting artifact.
- document-rule-dispositions.tsv is generated document-reachability data used by the parser
  and its certification suite.
- corrections.tsv and discrepancies.mec preserve stable specification decisions
  referenced by conformance cases.
- fixed-integer-intervals.md documents the current fixed-integer interval
  language, type, publication, and transport contract.
- acceptance-receipt.json records the selected pre-cleanup source and qualification
  evidence. It does not qualify later changes automatically.

Source tools consume canonical lossless syntax. The engine owns source semantics
and artifact construction; runtime owns activation, execution, resources, effects,
and host interfaces. See docs/design/canonical-source-boundary.mec.

Conformance
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Grammar changes update the specification, implementation mapping, generated
registries, and behavior cases together. The generation scripts offer --check to
verify current outputs. Recognition and typed-cast checks complement independent
semantic and product tests; a clean tree or artifact fingerprint alone is not
runtime behavior evidence.

Recovery evidence uses canonical syntax kinds, flags, source ranges, token text,
and normalized diagnostics. Codes, expected/found syntax, labels, fixes, and
recovery actions are structural contracts. Presentation wording and transient
snapshot identities are excluded.

Grammar notation
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Definitions use := and end with a semicolon. Sequence uses commas; alternatives
use |. Prefix ? means optional, * means zero-or-more, + means one-or-more,
¬ means excluded, and > means lookahead. Parentheses group expressions; the
existing two-operand square-bracket form denotes separated lists. Terminals
are quoted and terminal ranges use .. . Grammar names use kebab-case.

The grammar notation does not encode parser cuts, recovery, resource limits,
feature gates, or complete-consumption policy. Those belong to parser and
validation contracts.
